WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:08.960
We decided to look at an experiment that I had 

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.700
done as a postdoc,

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.370
where I had added a drug called brefeldin A 

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.850
that had been touted to block secretory trafficking.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.090
And when we added that drug,

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.450
we found that a particular subcompartment within the cell, 

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.890
called the Golgi apparatus,

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.460
disassembled.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:33.230
Essentially,

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.850
it redistributed its contents from the Golgi back 

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:38.850
into the ER.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:39.990
Now,

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.550
when I had made this discovery as a postdoc,

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.770
it was only done in fixed cells where we were just looking at fixed time 

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.740
points after the addition of the drug.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.490
It was an exciting result because the compartment that was disassembling, the 

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:02.950
Golgi apparatus, had previously been thought to be a 

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.650
stable structure that could not be created or destroyed within the 

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:08.000
cell.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:08.550
In fact,

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:09.940
as a graduate student,

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:14.650
I remember writing down in my notebook a quote from George Palade saying, 

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.840
the Golgi apparatus cannot be created or destroyed.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.510
It's like a mitochondria,

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.530
it's preexisting in some way.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.810
And I remember thinking,

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:25.960
well,

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.180
how do you really know that?

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:28.850
What's the proof for that?

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:33.430
And I was seeing as a postdoc in front of me,

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.970
a drug that caused that Golgi to disappear.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.850
So that went against a basic tenet that had been accepted in the 

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:41.250
field.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.290
So I knew that was going to be interesting to study.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:49.170
But it was very hard to study it when you were only using 

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:54.410
fixed materials because you didn't have a temporal aspect to the 

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:54.850
system.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.190
So as soon as it was clear that 

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:05.110
using fluorescent protein technology, where you could tag 

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:09.330
a protein of interest with a fluorescent beacon,

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:10.010
so to speak,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.670
a GFP molecule, and now track those molecules from one place to 

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:15.150
another,

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.650
I realized that we could finally watch 

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:25.650
how the Golgi apparatus disassembled in the presence of this drug, brefeldin A, 

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.900
and it was truly amazing.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.550
We knew it took on the order of about 10 minutes for the whole process to happen.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:39.870
But I wasn't expecting that it was going to happen in 

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:40.850
10 seconds.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:42.160
Basically,

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:44.880
when we were doing--

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:49.730
we essentially set up our microscope and was just doing continuous imaging and 

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.920
this was all done by hand at the time;

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.890
we didn't have fancy microscopes like we have now where you just program it and 

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:58.500
it starts taking the image,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:58.950
whatever,

00:02:59.740 --> 00:02:59.920
you know,

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.410
whatever time period you want.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:02.950
We were doing everything by hand.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.160
And I remember at one point, in about 19 seconds 

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:09.740
after adding this drug,

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.350
the Golgi sort of looked like it was going slightly out of focus,

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.210
and we started seeing these tubes spew out of this organelle.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.690
And within 10 seconds,

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.480
everything started blurring out and we saw all the material 

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.860
redistribute from the Golgi back into the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:33.670
It was absolutely mind blowing. The material in the Golgi, 

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:38.280
over a very short period, within about three or four seconds, 

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:43.390
was swept from the Golgi into the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.480
And that was the end of that organelle,

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:48.850
until we removed the drug.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.820
When BFA is washed out from cells,

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.150
the Golgi apparatus reassembles.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:59.150
This occurs as a result of Golgi enzymes that were trapped in the ER 

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:04.220
during brefeldin A treatment, now being able to be exported out of the ER 

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:09.210
into transport intermediates that fuse together to form a new 

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:10.020
Golgi structure.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.730
Although initially we didn't really know how it was working,

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:18.940
many people in the field subsequently then jumped into it to try to understand, 

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.640
what exactly is this drug doing?

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:26.850
And what we found, as a field, was that it's inhibiting 

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:32.110
a small GTPase, called Arf, that regulates the 

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.530
assembly of coat proteins onto the surface of membranes.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:41.350
And these were the same coat proteins that Rothman and Schekman 

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:46.040
had been talking about in their in vitro and genetic 

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:46.680
assays.

